    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d351">

                <pb facs="0797" n="769" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v01/pg769"/>

                <head>351. Telegram From the Commander, Military Assistance Command, Vietnam
                    (Westmoreland) to the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff (Wheeler)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d351-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Saigon</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1964-09-13"
                    >undated</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>MACV 26.</p>
                <p>1. General Duc just called and stated that he had understood that an individual
                    whose name was unfamiliar to me wanted to speak to him. I told him that I did
                    not know the individual or his whereabouts but I would talk with him. I asked if
                    he had seen Colonel Nemethy recently and he replied in the negative. He said he
                    was now at JGS Headquarters and had a few things to talk to General Khanh about
                    tomorrow. I asked him the status of the situation. He was very vague in his
                    reply. Then, in no uncertain terms, I informed him that MACV, the U.S. Mission,
                    and the U.S. Government did not support in any way his move. I advised that he
                    get his troops moved out of town immediately. He said he understood and thanked
                    me. He seemed to be a shaky and insecure young man.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d351-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d351-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Washington National Records Center, RG 319, HQDA Message Center, Reel
                    11925. Secret; Flash. Passed by the ICS to the Office of the Secretary of
                    Defense, the White House, and the Department of State. Also passed by other
                    means to the Embassy in Saigon. Received in the National Military Communications
                    Center at 3:30 p.m., September 13. No time or date of transmission appears on
                    the source text.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
